    <!-- Watch Out For -->
    <section class="exam-section" style="background: linear-gradient(135deg, #fff3cd 0%, #ffe5a0 100%); border-left-color: #ffc107;">
        <h2><i class="fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation section-icon" style="color: #856404;"></i> Common Misconceptions</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Binary Search:</strong> Students often forget that Binary Search requires a sorted list to function.</li>
            <li><strong>Sorting Efficiency:</strong> Believing Bubble Sort is "good" because it's easy to write, without understanding its $O(n^2)$ limitation.</li>
            <li><strong>File I/O:</strong> Forgetting to .close() a file or confusing 'w' (overwrite) with 'a' (append) modes.</li>
            <li><strong>Loops:</strong> Infinite loops caused by incorrect termination conditions in while structures.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- Big Ideas -->
    <section class="content-section" style="border-top-color: var(--accent-color);">
        <h2><i class="fa-solid fa-lightbulb section-icon"></i> Big Ideas to Consider</h2>
        <div class="learning-points">
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Reasoning and Decision-making:</strong> How does logical reasoning in code (Selection) mirror or differ from human decision-making? Does code "decide," or does it simply "follow"?</li>
                <li><strong>The Value of Efficiency:</strong> Is a more efficient algorithm "better" if it is harder for a human to maintain and debug?</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </section>

